 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Instability:** Directly training with a quantized bottleneck (CodecSplat) is unstable; the two-stage approach is required to stabilize Gaussian generation before introducing compression artifacts [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Resolution Mismatch:** In low-resolution stages, fine details are lost; the second stage must adequately refine these without getting stuck in local minima caused by the coarse initialization [S1].
-*   **Parameter Coupling:** In inverse rendering, failing to fix geometry in Stage 2 can lead to material/lighting ambiguity [S2].
+*   **Instability:** Directly training with a quantized bottleneck is unstable; the two-stage separation is required to stabilize Gaussian generation before introducing compression artifacts [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Gradient Conflicts:** Fixing parameters in Stage 2 (e.g., geometry in GS-IR) prevents conflicting gradient updates between geometry and material optimization [S2].
+*   **Rate Selection:** In CodecSplat, $\lambda$ must be sampled during training to cover the rate-distortion curve, but fixed at test time for a specific bit-rate [S3, unofficial].
 
